{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>The foreign gods of the land</b> — the nations of the land; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 31:16). But this is not so. Rather, it means the god who is foreign in this land, for HaShem the glorious is called \"the God of the land,\" as it is said (II Kings 17:26), \"for they knew not the judgment of the God of the land,\" and likewise, \"and they spoke of the God of Jerusalem as of the gods of the peoples of the land\" (II Chronicles 32:19), and it is written (Hosea 9:3), \"they shall not dwell in the land of YHWH.\" Rabbi Abraham mentioned this, and I have already explained it (Leviticus 18:25)." "And the sense of \"who comes there in its midst\" — that He comes in the midst of His people and His servants. Or <b>in its midst</b> — in the midst of the land, as in \"and the land did not bear them\" (Genesis 13:6), \"the land is dimmed\" (Isaiah 9:17), according to the words of Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 31:16)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The phrase אֱלֹהֵי נֵכַר הָאָרֶץ is ambiguous: 'foreign gods of the land' or 'the God who is foreign/unknown in the land.' Ramban reads it as the latter, referring to YHWH as the God whose worship is foreign to that land.", 1 "The verse phrase אֲשֶׁר הוּא בָּא שָׁמָּה בְּקִרְבּוֹ is read two ways: 'in its midst' referring to the people, or 'in its midst' referring to the land. The Isaiah reference in Hebrew reads טמן ארץ in some editions but the citation is to עתם ארץ; the translation reflects the cited verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:57:28.242940189Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}